# 24. Standards of evidence

Use the following hierarchy.

### Strongest

- complete independent proof;
- explicit counterexample;
- authoritative primary-source theorem matching the same assumptions;
- independently reproducible computation with clear provenance.

### Strong

- detailed proof reconstruction;
- multiple primary-source comparisons;
- independently implemented finite check.

### Moderate

- supplied computational evidence;
- supplied internal review;
- plausible but incomplete literature search.

### Weak

- undocumented assertion;
- search-result absence;
- internal novelty label;
- another agent saying "verified."

Calibrate conclusions accordingly.

---

# 25. Do not commit these audit errors

Do not:

- equate absence in a search with novelty;
- infer all-dimensional mathematics from finite enumeration;
- infer an operational algorithm from classical inspection of a symbolic state description;
- silently change oracle models;
- silently change scalar domains;
- apply field theorems to ring-valued models without proof;
- treat pairwise disjointness as sufficient for a multi-label direct sum;
- assume adaptivity is handled because the nonadaptive case works;
- ignore zero branches;
- ignore classical history as a potential information carrier;
- ignore ancillas;
- conflate QXOR upper bounds with arbitrary-\(G_0,G_1\) upper bounds;
- claim a Deutsch–Jozsa optimum that has not been proved;
- generalize the Simon scan;
- describe reproduced prior art as original;
- treat an internal agent review as independent peer review;
- treat a reproducible program as a proof of a quantified theorem.

